  "full_response": "The CAP theorem, also known as Brewer's theorem, states that a distributed system can only simultaneously provide two out of the following three guarantees: Consistency, Availability, and Partition tolerance. This theorem has profound implications for the design of distributed databases.\n\n\nConsistency refers to the guarantee that all nodes in the distributed system see the same data at the same. In a consistent system, when a transaction is made at one node, it will instantly be reflected across all other nodes.\n\n\nAvailability means that every request a response that has non-repudiation. Even in the case of a node failure, other nodes in the network can continue to serve requests.\n\n\nPartition tolerance implies the system continues to operate despite arbitrary message loss or delays between nodes, which is caused by network failures.\n\n\nImplications of the CAP theorem for distributed database design include:\n\n\n1. Trade-offs: Since a system cannot achieve all three properties simultaneously, database designers must make informed choices based on the specific needs of their applications.\n\n\n2. Consistency vs. Availability: For systems where the distribution network is expected to keep partitions to a minimum, engineers might prefer a system that opts for consistency. However, if having a high-availability system is crucial, and network partitions are common, availability may be prioritized. Systems such as Amazon's Dynamo make this trade-off by offering eventual consistency.\n\n\n3. Partition tolerance is assumed: The design assumes that partitions, though rare, are a part of networked systems for a prolonged period. Hence, applications are typically built to recover from or tolerate these events without losing critical functionality.\n\n\nThe CAP theorem serves as a guideline when architecting the behavior of distributed databases under the presence of network partitions or other disruptions. Each distributed system needs to consider the trade-offs between consistency, availability, and partition tolerance to ensure that it suits the requirements and context of its intended application.",
